[[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hazelgrove/hazel/issues/comments/1890393632","html_url":"https://github.com/hazelgrove/hazel/pull/1139#issuecomment-1890393632","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hazelgrove/hazel/issues/1139","id":1890393632,"node_id":"IC_kwDOBMdeZM5wrR4g","user":{"login":"pigumar1","id":108375845,"node_id":"U_kgDOBnWvJQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/108375845?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1","html_url":"https://github.com/pigumar1","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2024-01-13T09:29:20Z","updated_at":"2024-01-13T10:10:29Z","body":"Theoretical(?) basis:\r\nA case expression is necessarily exhaustive => (its corresponding final constraint |> truified |> dual) is inconsistent => no values of the given type satisfy it\r\n\r\nA case expression is necessarily inexhaustive => some value(s) of the given type satisfy it => there is some non-empty subset of final expressions of the given type that does not satisfy the final constraint, but satisfies its dual.\r\n\r\nNext we will show (**inductively**) that for a consistent dual (sometimes represented as a list of constraints here), there is always some constraint that, obviously, can be emitted from a single pattern that once that constraint is satisfied, the entire dual is satisfied. \r\n\r\n`Hole` constraints are truified, and `Falsity` constraints shouldn't appear. For simplicity, `Truth` constraints and repeated constraints are removed.\r\n\r\nBase case 1: the dual is a `Truth` constraint (the case expression has no branches at all). Any pattern of the given type can be used to satisfy the dual.\r\n\r\nBase case 2: the dual is a conjunction of `Int`/`NotInt` constraints.\r\nFor such dual to be consistent, the list should have at most 1 `Int` constraint, and others (if any) should be `NotInt`.\r\n* If there is exactly one `Int` constraint in the list, that constraint must be the one that if satisfied, the whole dual is satisfied. If we pick a different integer, it cannot satisfy that constraint, not to mention the entire dual.\r\n* (credit: @DavidFangWJ) If there is no `Int` constraint in the list, all the constraints in the list are going to be `NotInt`. We can pick an integer that is one larger than the largest integer among all associated with the `NotInt`s, which satisfies the dual. We can construct an `Int` constraint with it to denote this fact.\r\n* The same thing works for `Float`/`NotFloat` constraints and `String`/`NotString` constraints.\r\n\r\nRecursive cases:\r\n`And` constraint: can always be destructured to a list of constraints.\r\n![image](https://github.com/hazelgrove/hazel/assets/108375845/7695c10d-7eb5-412f-9892-67e876466856)\r\n\r\n`Or` constraint: for such dual to be consistent, there must be a constraint such that once it's satisfied, at least one of the \"subconstraints\" of the `Or` is satisfied, and it can be used to satisfy the entire dual.\r\n\r\nThe dual is a conjunction of `InjL` constraints: there must be some constraint `xi` s.t. if it's satisfied, all the \"unwrapped constraints\" are satisfied:\r\n![image](https://github.com/hazelgrove/hazel/assets/108375845/09286602-6655-4bd2-99e6-18837d5e0337)\r\nAnd `InjL(xi)` is the constraint that once satisfied, the entire dual will be satisfied.\r\nThe same thing works for `InjR` constraints.\r\n\r\nThe dual is a conjunction of `Pair` constraints: there must be at least one constraint `xi1` s.t. if it's satisfied, the \"0th projection constraint\" is satisfied. And there must be another constraint `xi2`. And `Pair(xi1, xi2)` is the constraint that once satisfied, the entire dual will be satisfied.","author_association":"MEMBER","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hazelgrove/hazel/issues/comments/1890393632/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hazelgrove/hazel/issues/comments/1890410358","html_url":"https://github.com/hazelgrove/hazel/pull/1139#issuecomment-1890410358","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/hazelgrove/hazel/issues/1139","id":1890410358,"node_id":"IC_kwDOBMdeZM5wrV92","user":{"login":"pigumar1","id":108375845,"node_id":"U_kgDOBnWvJQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/108375845?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1","html_url":"https://github.com/pigumar1","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pigumar1/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2024-01-13T10:27:27Z","updated_at":"2024-01-13T10:56:33Z","body":"If the basis above works, the next step would be to reverse the auxilliary 